In this lesson we'll look at how changing the three numbers in a quadratic changes its graph, one at a time. Picture a basic parabola, the U-shape from the square of x plus three. Now imagine three dials you can turn: a, b, and c. First, the dial on the square term, called a. If it's positive, the parabola opens upward like a smile; if it's negative, it opens downward like a frown. Crank the size of a up and the parabola gets skinny; shrink it down and it gets wide and stretched out. Now meet c, the easiest one. It's just where the graph crosses the up-and-down axis. Slide c up or down and the whole parabola slides with it, same shape, new height. Then there's b, the tricky one. Changing b doesn't tilt or squash the parabola, it slides it sideways, because b controls where the bottom or top of the parabola sits along the horizontal axis. Set b to zero and the parabola becomes perfectly symmetrical about the vertical axis. And here's a handy trick: rewrite the quadratic in vertex form, and the numbers that pop out tell you the turning point straight away. So three dials, three jobs: a flips and stretches, b slides sideways, c slides up and down. Now let's see it in action.